// 2017 GS1 (Prelims) Set A — Transcribed from "E:\UPSC question paper\2017\2017 prilims ques and answer.docx"
// Contains question, choices (A, B, C, D), answer, explanation, and upsc review points
export const paper = { year: 2017, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"With reference to the Parliament of India, consider the following statements:\nA private member's bill is a bill presented by a Member of Parliament who is not elected but only nominated by the President of India.\nRecently, a private member's bill has been passed in Parliament for the first time in its history.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Private Member’s Bill","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"A private member is any MP who is not a Minister.\nA private member may be an elected or nominated MP.\nPrivate Members' Bills have been passed earlier in Parliament.\nTherefore, both statements are incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Private Member: MP who is not a Minister.\nCan be elected or nominated.\nGovernment Bill: Introduced by a Minister.\nPrivate Members' Bills are generally introduced on Fridays."},
{"no":2,"text":"With reference to the difference between the culture of Rigvedic Aryans and Indus Valley people, which statements are correct?\nRigvedic Aryans used coat of mail and helmets in warfare, whereas Indus Valley people left no evidence of using them.\nRigvedic Aryans knew gold, silver and copper, whereas Indus Valley people knew only copper and iron.\nRigvedic Aryans had domesticated horses, whereas there is no conclusive evidence that Indus Valley people had.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Rigvedic Culture","Indus Valley Civilization","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Rigvedic sources indicate the use of protective warfare equipment such as helmets and coats of mail.\nArchaeological evidence does not establish the same level of evidence for the Indus Valley Civilization.\nRigvedic Aryans were familiar with horses.\nStatement 2 is incorrect because the Indus Valley people knew copper and bronze, among other metals; the statement incorrectly mentions iron.","revisionPoints":"Rigvedic culture: Strong association with cattle and horses.\nIndus Valley Civilization: Known for urban settlements and bronze technology.\nHorse evidence in the Indus Valley remains a debated archaeological issue.\nIron is not characteristic of mature Harappan metallurgy."},
{"no":3,"text":"Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL) Scheme refers to:","A":"Certifying the skills acquired by construction workers through traditional channels.","B":"Enrolling people in universities for distance learning.","C":"Reserving skilled jobs in public sector undertakings.","D":"Certifying skills acquired under NSDC training only.","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Labour & Employment","tags":["Skill India","RPL","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"RPL recognizes skills that individuals have already acquired through work or informal/traditional learning.\nIt provides assessment and certification of existing skills.\nIt is associated with the Skill India framework.\nIt is not restricted only to formal NSDC training.","revisionPoints":"RPL = Recognition of Prior Learning.\nFocuses on existing skills.\nUseful for workers who acquired skills through experience/informal learning.\nCertification can improve employability and mobility."},
{"no":4,"text":"From the ecological point of view, which one of the following assumes importance in being a good link between the Eastern Ghats and the Western Ghats?","A":"Sathyamangalam Tiger Reserve","B":"Nallamala Forest","C":"Nagarhole National Park","D":"Seshachalam Biosphere Reserve","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Sathyamangalam Tiger Reserve","Wildlife Corridor","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Sathyamangalam Tiger Reserve lies in Tamil Nadu.\nIt forms an important ecological connection between the Eastern Ghats and Western Ghats.\nSuch corridors facilitate movement of wildlife between fragmented habitats.\nIt is particularly important for large mammals such as elephants and tigers.","revisionPoints":"Located in Tamil Nadu.\nImportant wildlife corridor.\nConnects ecological landscapes of the Eastern and Western Ghats.\nImportant for tiger and elephant habitats."},
{"no":5,"text":"One of the implications of equality in society is the absence of:","A":"Privileges","B":"Restraints","C":"Competition","D":"Ideology","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Political Theory","tags":["Equality","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Equality implies that individuals should not enjoy special privileges merely because of status or birth.\nIt promotes equal treatment and equal opportunity.\nEquality does not necessarily eliminate competition.\nTherefore, absence of privileges is the appropriate answer.","revisionPoints":"Equality ≠ identical treatment in every situation.\nPolitical equality → equal political rights.\nSocial equality → absence of status-based discrimination.\nEquality of opportunity is a core democratic principle."},
{"no":6,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding TRAFFIC:\nTRAFFIC is a bureau under UNEP.\nTRAFFIC ensures that trade in wild plants and animals is not a threat to conservation.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"International Environmental Bodies","tags":["TRAFFIC","IUCN","WWF","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"TRAFFIC works on issues concerning wildlife trade and conservation.\nIt was jointly established by WWF and IUCN.\nIt is not a bureau under UNEP.\nIts work seeks to ensure that wildlife trade does not threaten conservation.","revisionPoints":"TRAFFIC: Wildlife trade monitoring network.\nAssociated with WWF and IUCN.\nFocus: illegal and unsustainable wildlife trade.\nImportant distinction: TRAFFIC ≠ UNEP bureau."},
{"no":7,"text":"Which principle was added to the Directive Principles by the 42nd Constitutional Amendment?","A":"Equal pay for equal work","B":"Workers' participation in management","C":"Right to work, education and public assistance","D":"Living wage and humane conditions","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitutional Amendment","tags":["42nd Amendment","DPSP","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1976 added Article 43A.\nArticle 43A deals with participation of workers in management of industries.\nThe other principles mentioned were already part of the Directive Principles.","revisionPoints":"Article 43A: Workers' participation in management.\nAdded by the 42nd Amendment, 1976.\nLocated in Part IV.\nDPSPs are contained in Articles 36–51."},
{"no":8,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Rights are claims of the State against citizens.","B":"Rights are privileges incorporated in the Constitution.","C":"Rights are claims of citizens against the State.","D":"Rights are privileges of a few citizens.","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Political Theory","tags":["Rights","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Rights protect individuals against arbitrary exercise of state power.\nFundamental Rights place constitutional limitations on the State.\nThey enable citizens to demand protection and respect for their liberties.\nTherefore, the correct statement is that rights constitute claims of citizens against the State.","revisionPoints":"Fundamental Rights → Part III.\nArticles 12–35.\nProtect individual liberty and equality.\nSome rights are available only to citizens, while others are available to all persons."},
{"no":9,"text":"Which organization publishes the Global Gender Gap Index?","A":"World Economic Forum","B":"UN Human Rights Council","C":"UN Women","D":"World Health Organization","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["World Economic Forum","Gender Gap Index","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The World Economic Forum (WEF) publishes the Global Gender Gap Report.\nThe report compares countries across dimensions such as economic participation, education, health and political empowerment.\nIt is an international comparative index on gender disparities.","revisionPoints":"Publisher: World Economic Forum.\nMeasures gender gaps, not simply women's welfare.\nMajor dimensions:\nEconomic participation\nEducational attainment\nHealth and survival\nPolitical empowerment"},
{"no":10,"text":"Which of the following statements are correct regarding Smart India Hackathon 2017?\nCentrally Sponsored Scheme for developing smart cities.\nInitiative to identify digital technology innovations.\nProgramme to make all financial transactions digital.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"3 only","D":"2 and 3 only","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Smart India Hackathon","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Smart India Hackathon was designed to encourage innovative solutions using technology.\nIt involved solving real-world problems through student innovation.\nIt was not a scheme specifically for developing smart cities.\nIt was also not a programme aimed at making all financial transactions digital.","revisionPoints":"Focus: innovation and problem-solving.\nMajor participation by students and educational institutions.\nUses technology to address real-world challenges.\nDistinguish from the Smart Cities Mission."},
{"no":11,"text":"Regarding the Monetary Policy Committee (MPC), consider the following:\nIt decides RBI's benchmark interest rate.\nIt is a 12-member body.\nIt functions under the Union Finance Minister.","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"3 only","D":"2 and 3 only","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Monetary Policy Committee","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The MPC determines the policy interest rate needed to achieve the inflation target.\nThe committee has 6 members, not 12.\nIt is chaired by the RBI Governor.\nIt does not function under the Union Finance Minister.","revisionPoints":"MPC = 6 members.\nChairperson → RBI Governor.\nDetermines the repo rate as the policy rate.\nCreated under the RBI Act framework.\nMonetary policy → primarily concerned with inflation and liquidity/interest rates."},
{"no":12,"text":"With reference to Manipuri Sankirtana, consider the following statements:\nIt is a song and dance performance.\nCymbals are the only musical instruments used.\nIt narrates the life and deeds of Lord Krishna.","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1 only","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Music","tags":["Manipuri Sankirtana","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Manipuri Sankirtana combines singing, music and dance.\nIt is associated with the Vaishnavite tradition of Manipur.\nThemes include the life and deeds of Lord Krishna.\nCymbals are used, but they are not the only instruments.","revisionPoints":"Associated with Manipur.\nCombines music, singing and dance.\nStrong association with Vaishnavism.\nKrishna-related themes are prominent."},
{"no":13,"text":"Who among the following were associated with the introduction of Ryotwari Settlement?\nLord Cornwallis\nAlexander Read\nThomas Munro","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Ryotwari Settlement","Thomas Munro","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Alexander Read experimented with the Ryotwari system.\nThomas Munro played a major role in developing and implementing it.\nLord Cornwallis is primarily associated with the Permanent Settlement.\nHence, statements 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Ryotwari: Direct settlement between government and cultivator.\nAssociated with Thomas Munro and Alexander Read.\nProminent in Madras and Bombay Presidencies.\nPermanent Settlement: Lord Cornwallis.\nMahalwari: Settlement with village/community or estate."},
{"no":14,"text":"What are the advantages of bioremediation?\nCleans pollution using natural biodegradation.\nHeavy metals like cadmium and lead can be treated using microorganisms.\nGenetic engineering can create microorganisms for bioremediation.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Technology","tags":["Bioremediation","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Bioremediation uses living organisms or their biological processes to remove or degrade pollutants.\nMicroorganisms can be genetically modified to improve their ability to degrade specific pollutants.\nThe source identifies statements 1 and 3 as correct.\nHence, the correct answer is 1 and 3 only.","revisionPoints":"Bioremediation: Biological treatment of pollution.\nCommon agents: bacteria, fungi and plants.\nUsed for contaminated soil and water.\nGenetic engineering can enhance remediation capabilities."},
{"no":15,"text":"The Trade Disputes Act, 1929 provided for:","A":"Workers' participation in management","B":"Arbitrary powers to management","C":"Intervention by British Courts","D":"Tribunals and restrictions on strikes","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Labour & Employment","tags":["Trade Disputes Act 1929","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The Trade Disputes Act, 1929 was part of the colonial government's approach to industrial disputes.\nIt provided mechanisms such as trade dispute tribunals.\nIt also imposed restrictions on certain strikes.\nTherefore, option (d) is correct.","revisionPoints":"Enacted during the British colonial period.\nConcerned with industrial disputes.\nIncluded restrictions on strikes.\nImportant in the evolution of Indian labour legislation."},
{"no":16,"text":"Local self-government is best explained as an exercise in:","A":"Federalism","B":"Democratic decentralisation","C":"Administrative delegation","D":"Direct democracy","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Local Self-Government","tags":["Democratic Decentralisation","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Local self-government transfers decision-making closer to the people.\nIt enables citizens to participate in governance at the grassroots level.\nTherefore, it represents democratic decentralisation.\nThe 73rd and 74th Constitutional Amendments strengthened this framework.","revisionPoints":"73rd Amendment: Panchayati Raj institutions.\n74th Amendment: Urban local bodies.\nLocal government → grassroots democracy.\nDecentralisation improves local participation and accountability."},
{"no":17,"text":"The Directive Principles of State Policy constitute limitations upon:\nLegislative function\nExecutive function","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Directive Principles of State Policy","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"DPSPs guide the legislature in making laws.\nThey also guide the executive in framing and implementing policies.\nThey are not enforceable by courts in the same manner as Fundamental Rights.\nNevertheless, they provide constitutional direction to both legislative and executive action.","revisionPoints":"DPSPs → Part IV.\nArticles 36–51.\nNon-justiciable.\nAim to establish a social and economic democracy.\nGuide both law-making and governance."},
{"no":18,"text":"\"Digital Single Market Strategy\" refers to:","A":"ASEAN","B":"BRICS","C":"European Union","D":"G20","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["Digital Single Market Strategy","European Union","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The Digital Single Market Strategy is associated with the European Union.\nIt aims to reduce digital barriers among EU member states.\nIt focuses on creating a more integrated digital economy across the EU.","revisionPoints":"Associated with the European Union.\nFocuses on digital integration.\nSeeks to reduce barriers to cross-border digital activity.\nRelevant areas include digital services, e-commerce and data-related regulation."},
{"no":19,"text":"The place in India where sea water recedes several kilometres during low tide is:","A":"Bhavnagar","B":"Bheemunipatnam","C":"Chandipur","D":"Nagapattinam","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Chandipur","Tidal Recession","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Chandipur is located on the coast of Odisha.\nIt is famous for its unusual tidal phenomenon.\nDuring low tide, seawater can recede several kilometres from the shoreline.\nThe phenomenon exposes a broad stretch of the seabed.","revisionPoints":"Chandipur → Odisha.\nLocated on the Bay of Bengal coast.\nFamous for large tidal recession.\nAlso known for the Integrated Test Range."},
{"no":20,"text":"Regarding the Prohibition of Benami Property Transactions Act, consider the following:\nA property is not benami if the owner is unaware of the transaction.\nBenami properties can be confiscated.\nThe Act provides authorities for investigation but no appellate mechanism.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"2 and 3 only","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["Benami Transactions Act","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The Act provides for confiscation of benami properties.\nStatement 1 does not correctly describe the statutory definition of benami property.\nThe Act also provides an appellate mechanism, making statement 3 incorrect.\nTherefore, only statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Benami transaction: Property held by one person while consideration is provided by another, subject to statutory exceptions.\nBenami property can be attached and confiscated under the law.\nAuthorities include the Initiating Officer and Adjudicating Authority.\nThere is an appellate mechanism.\nUPSC – Questions 21–40"},
{"no":21,"text":"If there is a huge decline in butterfly populations, what could be the consequences?\nPollination of some plants could be affected.\nIncrease in fungal infections in cultivated plants.\nDecline in populations of wasps, spiders and birds.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Butterflies","Pollination","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Butterflies act as pollinators for several plant species.\nTheir decline can therefore affect pollination and plant reproduction.\nButterflies also form part of food webs.\nA decline can affect predators such as wasps, spiders and birds.\nThe source does not identify increased fungal infections as a consequence.","revisionPoints":"Butterflies → important pollinators.\nThey are part of the food chain.\nBiodiversity decline can cause cascading ecological effects.\nPollinators include butterflies, bees, moths and some birds."},
{"no":22,"text":"What are the likely limitations of promoting algae-based biofuels?\nProduction is possible only in seas.\nProduction requires high expertise and technology.\nLarge-scale production may create ecological and social concerns.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["Algae Biofuels","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Algae can be cultivated in different environments and are not restricted only to seas.\nLarge-scale production requires significant technical expertise and infrastructure.\nLarge-scale cultivation can create ecological and social concerns.\nHence, statements 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Algae can be used to produce biofuels.\nPotential advantages include high productivity and use of certain non-arable areas.\nChallenges include technology, cost and scaling.\nLarge-scale cultivation may have ecological implications."},
{"no":23,"text":"Which of the following are objectives of the National Nutrition Mission?\nCreate awareness about malnutrition.\nReduce anaemia among women and children.\nPromote consumption of millets.\nPromote consumption of poultry eggs.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1, 2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 4 only","D":"3 and 4 only","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["National Nutrition Mission","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The National Nutrition Mission focuses on addressing malnutrition.\nImproving awareness regarding nutrition is an important component.\nReducing anaemia among women and children is also a stated objective.\nThe source does not identify promotion of millets or poultry eggs as the objectives tested here.","revisionPoints":"National Nutrition Mission is associated with improving nutrition outcomes.\nFocus areas include:\nMalnutrition\nAnaemia\nMaternal and child nutrition\nConvergence among different government programmes is important."},
{"no":24,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe Factories Act, 1881 was passed to fix wages and allow trade unions.\nN.M. Lokhande was a pioneer in organizing the labour movement in British India.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Factories Act 1881","N.M. Lokhande","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"N.M. Lokhande was an important pioneer of the labour movement in colonial India.\nThe statement about the Factories Act, 1881 incorrectly describes its purpose.\nTherefore, only statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"N.M. Lokhande → important early labour leader.\nAssociated with organisation of workers in Bombay.\nEarly factory legislation focused on working conditions and regulation of factories.\nLabour movements developed alongside industrialisation."},
{"no":25,"text":"In the context of carbon sequestration, which of the following can be potential sites?\nAbandoned and uneconomic coal seams\nDepleted oil and gas reservoirs\nSubterranean deep saline formations","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Carbon Sequestration","CCS","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"All three are potential geological formations for long-term CO₂ storage.\nUneconomic coal seams can retain carbon dioxide.\nDepleted oil and gas reservoirs can provide underground storage.\nDeep saline formations are also important potential storage sites.","revisionPoints":"Carbon sequestration: Capture and long-term storage of carbon dioxide.\nImportant geological storage options:\nCoal seams\nDepleted oil/gas reservoirs\nDeep saline formations\nForms part of Carbon Capture and Storage (CCS).\nObjective → reduce atmospheric CO₂ emissions."},
{"no":26,"text":"The object of the Butler Committee (1927) was to:","A":"Define the jurisdiction of the Central and Provincial Governments.","B":"Define the powers of the Secretary of State for India.","C":"Impose censorship on national press.","D":"Improve the relationship between the Government of India and the Indian States.","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Butler Committee","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The Butler Committee was appointed in 1927.\nIt examined the relationship between the British Government and the princely states.\nIts focus was particularly on the relationship involving paramountcy.","revisionPoints":"Butler Committee → 1927.\nConcerned with princely states.\nExamined British paramountcy.\nDistinguish it from committees dealing with constitutional reforms in British provinces."},
{"no":27,"text":"The term \"Domestic Content Requirement\" is sometimes seen in the news with reference to:","A":"Developing solar power production in our country.","B":"Granting licences to foreign TV channels.","C":"Exporting food products.","D":"Allowing foreign universities in India.","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Trade","tags":["Domestic Content Requirement","National Solar Mission","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Domestic Content Requirement required certain solar projects to use domestically manufactured solar cells/modules.\nIt was associated with India's National Solar Mission.\nThe objective was to encourage domestic manufacturing capabilities.","revisionPoints":"DCR → preference/requirement for domestic manufacturing.\nAssociated with solar energy policy.\nConnected with development of India's domestic solar manufacturing sector.\nRelevant to debates concerning trade rules and WTO obligations."},
{"no":28,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe Nuclear Security Summits are periodically held under the aegis of the United Nations.\nThe International Panel on Fissile Materials is an organ of the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA).","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Nuclear Technology","tags":["Nuclear Security Summit","IPFM","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Nuclear Security Summits were not conducted under the aegis of the UN.\nThe International Panel on Fissile Materials (IPFM) is an independent research group.\nIt is not an organ of the IAEA.","revisionPoints":"Nuclear Security Summit: International diplomatic process focused on nuclear security.\nIAEA: Major international organisation dealing with peaceful nuclear energy and safeguards.\nIPFM: Independent expert/research group.\nDo not automatically associate every nuclear initiative with the IAEA or UN."},
{"no":29,"text":"Who among the following can join the National Pension System (NPS)?","A":"Resident Indian citizens only.","B":"Persons of age from 21 to 55 years only.","C":"All State Government employees joining service after notification by the respective State Governments.","D":"All Central Government employees including Armed Forces joining on or after 1 April 2004.","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["National Pension System","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"NPS applies to State Government employees after the respective State Government adopts/notifies the system.\nThe source specifically notes that the Armed Forces were initially excluded.\nTherefore, option (c) is the correct answer according to the source.","revisionPoints":"NPS → defined contribution pension system.\nImplemented for Central Government employees from 2004.\nState Government coverage depends on adoption by the respective State.\nPension systems can have different rules for different categories of employees."},
{"no":30,"text":"With reference to River Teesta, consider the following statements:\nThe source of River Teesta is the same as that of Brahmaputra.\nRiver Rangeet originates in Sikkim and is a tributary of Teesta.\nRiver Teesta flows into the Bay of Bengal on the India-Bangladesh border.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["River Teesta","Rangeet","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Rangeet is a major tributary of the Teesta.\nTeesta and Brahmaputra have different sources.\nTeesta joins the Brahmaputra in Bangladesh rather than directly flowing into the Bay of Bengal at the India-Bangladesh border.\nTherefore, only statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Teesta → major river of Sikkim and West Bengal.\nRangeet → tributary of Teesta.\nTeesta eventually joins the Brahmaputra/Jamuna system.\nImportant for India-Bangladesh water-sharing relations."},
{"no":31,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nIn tropical regions, Zika virus disease is transmitted by the same mosquito that transmits dengue.\nSexual transmission of Zika virus disease is possible.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Zika Virus","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Zika is primarily transmitted by Aedes mosquitoes.\nAedes mosquitoes are also vectors of dengue.\nZika can also be transmitted through sexual contact.\nTherefore, both statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"Zika → viral disease.\nImportant vector → Aedes mosquitoes.\nSexual transmission is possible.\nOther mosquito-borne diseases involving Aedes include dengue and chikungunya."},
{"no":32,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nBIS certification is mandatory for automotive tyres and tubes.\nAGMARK is a quality certification mark issued by FAO.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Regulation","tags":["BIS Certification","AGMARK","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"BIS certification is mandatory for specified automotive tyres and tubes.\nAGMARK is an Indian quality certification system.\nAGMARK is not issued by the FAO.\nTherefore, only statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"BIS → Bureau of Indian Standards.\nBIS deals with standards and certification in India.\nAGMARK → agricultural products.\nAGMARK is associated with the Government of India, not FAO."},
{"no":33,"text":"What are the advantages of implementing the National Agriculture Market (e-NAM)?\nIt is a pan-India electronic trading portal.\nIt provides nationwide market access with better price discovery.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["e-NAM","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"e-NAM is an electronic platform intended to integrate agricultural markets.\nIt provides farmers and traders with access to a broader market.\nElectronic trading can improve price discovery and transparency.\nHence, both statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"e-NAM = National Agriculture Market.\nPan-India electronic agricultural trading platform.\nFocus:\nMarket integration\nTransparency\nBetter price discovery\nHelps connect participating APMC markets."},
{"no":34,"text":"With reference to the National Intellectual Property Rights Policy, consider the following statements:\nIt reiterates India's commitment to the Doha Development Agenda and TRIPS Agreement.\nDepartment of Industrial Policy and Promotion (DIPP) is the nodal agency for regulating IPR in India.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Intellectual Property","tags":["National IPR Policy","TRIPS","DIPP","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The National IPR Policy recognises India's commitments under international frameworks including TRIPS.\nThe source identifies DIPP as the nodal agency in the policy framework.\nTherefore, both statements are correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"IPR: Intellectual Property Rights.\nMajor categories:\nPatents\nCopyright\nTrademarks\nGeographical indications\nDesigns\nTRIPS → WTO agreement concerning intellectual property.\nDoha Development Agenda → WTO negotiations framework."},
{"no":35,"text":"According to the Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972, which of the following animals cannot be hunted except under certain legal provisions?\nGharial\nIndian Wild Ass\nWild Buffalo","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Law","tags":["Wildlife Protection Act 1972","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The listed animals receive legal protection under the wildlife protection framework.\nHunting of protected wildlife is generally prohibited except under specified statutory circumstances.\nThe source identifies all three animals as covered by the relevant protection.","revisionPoints":"Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972 → key Indian wildlife legislation.\nHunting of protected species is generally prohibited.\nGharial → critically endangered crocodilian.\nIndian Wild Ass → associated strongly with Gujarat.\nWild Buffalo → threatened wild bovine species."},
{"no":36,"text":"Which of the following statements are true regarding Fundamental Duties?\nA legislative process has been provided to enforce these duties.\nThey are correlative to legal duties.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Fundamental Duties","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Fundamental Duties are contained in Article 51A.\nThey are not directly enforceable in the same manner as Fundamental Rights.\nHowever, Parliament can enact laws giving effect to particular duties.\nThe source gives the Prevention of Insults to National Honour Act as an example.","revisionPoints":"Fundamental Duties → Article 51A.\nAdded by the 42nd Amendment, 1976.\nOriginally 10 duties.\n11th duty was added by the 86th Amendment.\nDuties are generally non-justiciable directly."},
{"no":37,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nRadhakanta Deb — First President of the British Indian Association.\nGazulu Lakshminarasu Chetty — Founder of the Madras Mahajana Sabha.\nSurendranath Banerjee — Founder of the Indian Association.","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["British Indian Association","Madras Mahajana Sabha","Indian Association","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Radhakanta Deb was associated with the British Indian Association.\nSurendranath Banerjee was a founder of the Indian Association.\nMadras Mahajana Sabha was founded by M. Veeraraghavachariar, G. Subramania Iyer and P. Anandacharlu.\nTherefore, pair 2 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Indian Association → 1876.\nAssociated with Surendranath Banerjee and Ananda Mohan Bose.\nMadras Mahajana Sabha → 1884.\nEarly political associations contributed to the growth of Indian nationalism."},
{"no":38,"text":"Which one of the following objectives is not embodied in the Preamble to the Constitution of India?","A":"Liberty of thought","B":"Economic liberty","C":"Liberty of expression","D":"Liberty of belief","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Preamble","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The Preamble explicitly mentions liberty of thought, expression, belief, faith and worship.\nIt does not expressly mention economic liberty.\nTherefore, economic liberty is the correct answer.","revisionPoints":"Preamble mentions:\nJustice – social, economic, political\nLiberty – thought, expression, belief, faith, worship\nEquality – status and opportunity\nFraternity\nEconomic justice is mentioned, but not \"economic liberty\".\nPreamble reflects the philosophy and objectives of the Constitution."},
{"no":39,"text":"With reference to the \"Quality Council of India (QCI)\", consider the following statements:\nQCI was set up jointly by the Government of India and the Indian Industry.\nThe Chairman of QCI is appointed by the Prime Minister on the recommendation of the industry to the Government.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["Quality Council of India","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"QCI was established through a joint initiative involving the Government of India and Indian industry.\nThe source states that the Chairman is not appointed by the Prime Minister in the manner described in statement 2.\nHence, only statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"QCI = Quality Council of India.\nAssociated with quality standards and accreditation.\nJoint initiative of government and industry.\nImportant in promoting quality infrastructure and accreditation systems."},
{"no":40,"text":"What is the purpose of setting up of Small Finance Banks (SFBs) in India?\nTo supply credit to small business units.\nTo supply credit to small and marginal farmers.\nTo encourage young entrepreneurs to set up businesses particularly in rural areas.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Small Finance Banks","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Small Finance Banks were established primarily to promote financial inclusion.\nThey provide credit to small businesses and small/marginal farmers.\nThey also support micro and small industries and the unorganised sector.\nThe source includes young entrepreneurs among the intended beneficiaries.","revisionPoints":"SFBs → financial inclusion.\nTarget groups include:\nSmall businesses\nSmall and marginal farmers\nMicro and small industries\nUnorganised sector\nSFBs accept deposits and provide loans.\nFocus is on underserved sections and areas.\nUPSC – Questions 41–60"},
{"no":41,"text":"With reference to Asia Pacific Ministerial Conference on Housing and Urban Development (APMCHUD), consider the following statements:\nThe first APMCHUD was held in India in 2006 on the theme \"Emerging Urban Forms – Policy Responses and Governance Structure\".\nIndia hosts all the Annual Ministerial Conferences in partnership with ADB, APEC and ASEAN.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["APMCHUD","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The first APMCHUD was hosted by India in 2006.\nIts theme was \"Emerging Urban Forms – Policy Responses and Governance Structure.\"\nIndia does not permanently host all subsequent conferences.\nTherefore, only statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"APMCHUD → Asia-Pacific regional platform.\nFirst conference → India, 2006.\nFocus → housing and sustainable urban development.\nDo not confuse it with permanent hosting by India."},
{"no":42,"text":"Democracy's superior virtue lies in the fact that it calls into activity:","A":"The intelligence and character of ordinary men and women.","B":"The methods for strengthening executive leadership.","C":"A superior individual with dynamism and vision.","D":"A band of dedicated party workers.","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Political Theory","tags":["Democracy","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Democracy encourages participation of ordinary citizens in public affairs.\nIt provides opportunities for citizens to exercise judgement and responsibility.\nDemocratic governance therefore depends upon active and informed citizens.\nHence, option (a) is correct.","revisionPoints":"Democracy → popular participation.\nCitizens are participants, not merely subjects.\nDemocratic institutions depend upon public awareness and responsibility.\nPolitical participation is broader than merely voting."},
{"no":43,"text":"Which of the following is a most likely consequence of implementing the \"Unified Payments Interface (UPI)\"?","A":"Mobile wallets will not be necessary.","B":"Digital currency will totally replace physical currency within two decades.","C":"FDI inflows will drastically increase.","D":"Direct transfer of subsidies to poor people will become very effective.","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["UPI","Direct Benefit Transfer","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"UPI enables fast and interoperable digital fund transfers.\nIt can improve the efficiency of Direct Benefit Transfers (DBT).\nUPI does not automatically eliminate the need for wallets.\nIt also does not imply that physical currency will completely disappear.","revisionPoints":"UPI → Unified Payments Interface.\nEnables real-time bank-to-bank payments.\nSupports digital financial inclusion.\nCan facilitate efficient DBT and digital payments.\nOperated within India's digital payments ecosystem through NPCI."},
{"no":44,"text":"The terms \"Event Horizon\", \"Singularity\", \"String Theory\" and \"Standard Model\" are seen in the news in the context of:","A":"Observation and understanding of the Universe.","B":"Study of solar and lunar eclipses.","C":"Placing satellites in Earth's orbit.","D":"Origin and evolution of living organisms.","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Event Horizon","String Theory","Standard Model","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Event horizon and singularity are associated with black holes and gravity.\nString theory attempts to describe fundamental particles and forces.\nThe Standard Model describes fundamental particles and their interactions.\nThese concepts belong to modern physics and the study of the universe.","revisionPoints":"Event horizon: Boundary beyond which escape from a black hole is not possible classically.\nSingularity: Region where classical descriptions of gravity break down.\nStandard Model: Framework for fundamental particles and interactions.\nString theory: Theoretical framework involving fundamental strings."},
{"no":45,"text":"With reference to agriculture in India, how can the technique of \"Genome Sequencing\" be used in the immediate future?\nIdentify genetic markers for disease resistance and drought tolerance.\nReduce the time required to develop new crop varieties.\nDecipher post-harvest relationships in crops.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Genome Sequencing","Crop Breeding","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Genome sequencing helps identify genes and genetic markers associated with useful traits.\nThis can accelerate crop breeding and development of improved varieties.\nIt can also improve understanding of crop characteristics and their relationships.\nTherefore, all three statements are considered correct in the source.","revisionPoints":"Genome sequencing → determining the DNA sequence of an organism.\nAgricultural applications:\nDisease resistance\nDrought tolerance\nYield-related traits\nFaster breeding\nSupports precision breeding and crop improvement."},
{"no":46,"text":"The main advantage of the parliamentary form of government is that:","A":"Executive and Legislature work independently.","B":"It provides continuity of policy and is more efficient.","C":"Executive remains responsible to the Legislature.","D":"Head of Government cannot be changed without election.","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Parliamentary Government","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"In a parliamentary system, the executive is collectively responsible to the legislature.\nThe Council of Ministers must retain the confidence of the lower House.\nThis creates an important mechanism of legislative accountability.\nTherefore, option (c) is correct.","revisionPoints":"India follows a parliamentary system.\nCouncil of Ministers → collectively responsible to Lok Sabha.\nPrime Minister → real executive head.\nPresident → constitutional/formal head.\nExecutive accountability is a key feature."},
{"no":47,"text":"In the context of India, which one of the following is the correct relationship between Rights and Duties?","A":"Rights are correlative with Duties.","B":"Rights are personal and independent of society and Duties.","C":"Rights, not Duties, are important.","D":"Duties, not Rights, are important.","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Rights and Duties","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Rights and duties are closely connected in a democratic society.\nEnjoyment of rights involves respecting the rights of others.\nFundamental Duties reinforce civic responsibility.\nTherefore, rights and duties are considered correlative.","revisionPoints":"Rights → entitlements/protections.\nDuties → responsibilities.\nRights cannot be viewed completely independently of social obligations.\nFundamental Duties are contained in Article 51A."},
{"no":48,"text":"The mind of the makers of the Constitution of India is reflected in:","A":"The Preamble","B":"The Fundamental Rights","C":"The Directive Principles of State Policy","D":"The Fundamental Duties","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Preamble","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The Preamble expresses the philosophy and objectives underlying the Constitution.\nIt reflects ideals such as justice, liberty, equality and fraternity.\nIt therefore provides a concise expression of the constitutional vision.","revisionPoints":"Preamble → introductory statement of constitutional philosophy.\nKey ideals:\nJustice\nLiberty\nEquality\nFraternity\nIt helps understand the objectives of the Constitution."},
{"no":49,"text":"If you travel by road from Kohima to Kottayam, what is the minimum number of States through which you travel, including the origin and destination?","A":"6","B":"7","C":"8","D":"9","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["States of India","Kohima","Kottayam","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"According to the source, the shortest route passes through:\nNagaland\nAssam\nWest Bengal\nOdisha\nAndhra Pradesh\nTamil Nadu\nKerala\nTherefore, the minimum number is 7 States.","revisionPoints":"Origin → Kohima, Nagaland.\nDestination → Kottayam, Kerala.\nRoute questions test knowledge of Indian geography and spatial connectivity.\nRemember that the question asks to include both origin and destination."},
{"no":50,"text":"The Parliament of India exercises control over the Council of Ministers through:\nAdjournment Motion\nQuestion Hour\nSupplementary Questions","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Question Hour","Adjournment Motion","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Parliament holds the executive accountable through various parliamentary devices.\nQuestion Hour enables MPs to question Ministers.\nSupplementary Questions allow further clarification.\nAn Adjournment Motion can draw attention to a definite matter of urgent public importance.","revisionPoints":"Parliamentary control mechanisms include:\nQuestion Hour\nSupplementary Questions\nAdjournment Motion\nNo-Confidence Motion\nCalling Attention\nCouncil of Ministers is responsible to the Lok Sabha.\nParliamentary questions promote executive accountability."},
{"no":51,"text":"Which one of the following was a very important seaport in the Kakatiya kingdom?","A":"Kakinada","B":"Motupalli","C":"Machilipatnam (Masulipatnam)","D":"Nelluru","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Kakatiya Kingdom","Motupalli","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Motupalli was an important international seaport under the Kakatiya dynasty.\nIt played an important role in maritime trade.\nThe port connected the Kakatiya kingdom with overseas commercial networks.","revisionPoints":"Motupalli → Kakatiya kingdom.\nLocated on the Andhra coast.\nImportant for international maritime trade.\nKakatiyas were based primarily in the Telangana-Andhra region."},
{"no":52,"text":"With reference to the \"Global Climate Change Alliance (GCCA)\", which statements are correct?\nIt is an initiative of the European Union.\nIt provides technical and financial support to developing countries.\nIt is coordinated by the World Resources Institute (WRI) and WBCSD.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Global Climate Change Alliance","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"GCCA is an initiative of the European Union.\nIt supports developing countries through technical and financial assistance related to climate change.\nStatement 3 refers to a different initiative/framework.\nHence, only statements 1 and 2 are correct.","revisionPoints":"GCCA → European Union initiative.\nFocus → climate change cooperation with developing countries.\nSupport includes technical and financial assistance.\nDistinguish GCCA from other climate partnerships."},
{"no":53,"text":"With reference to the religious history of India, consider the following statements:\nSautrantika and Sammitiya were sects of Jainism.\nSarvastivadin believed that the constituents of phenomena existed in the past, present and future.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Buddhism","Sarvastivadin","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Sautrantika and Sammitiya were schools/traditions associated with Buddhism, not Jainism.\nSarvastivada is associated with the idea that phenomena or dharmas have existence across past, present and future.\nTherefore, only statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Sautrantika → Buddhist school.\nSammitiya → Buddhist school.\nSarvastivada → Buddhist tradition.\n\"Sarva asti\" is associated with the idea of existence of dharmas in three times."},
{"no":54,"text":"Mediterranean Sea is a border of which countries?\nJordan\nIraq\nLebanon\nSyria","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 and 4 only","D":"1, 3 and 4 only","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Mediterranean Sea","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Lebanon has a Mediterranean coastline.\nSyria also has a Mediterranean coastline.\nJordan and Iraq are landlocked and do not border the Mediterranean Sea.\nTherefore, statements 3 and 4 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Mediterranean coastline in the Levant includes:\nLebanon\nSyria\nIsrael\nJordan → no Mediterranean coastline.\nIraq → no Mediterranean coastline.\nGeography questions often test coastal vs landlocked countries."},
{"no":55,"text":"With reference to the National Investment and Infrastructure Fund (NIIF), which statements are correct?\nIt is an organ of NITI Aayog.\nIt has a corpus of ₹40,000 crore at present.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["National Investment and Infrastructure Fund","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"NIIF is an investment fund associated with the Department of Economic Affairs, not an organ of NITI Aayog.\nThe source identifies the corpus as ₹40,000 crore in the context of the question.\nTherefore, statement 2 is correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"NIIF → National Investment and Infrastructure Fund.\nFocus → infrastructure and investment.\nIt is not an organ of NITI Aayog.\nAssociated with the Department of Economic Affairs."},
{"no":56,"text":"The Global Infrastructure Facility (GIF) is:","A":"ASEAN initiative","B":"World Bank collaboration supporting PPP infrastructure projects","C":"OECD banking collaboration","D":"UNCTAD initiative","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["Global Infrastructure Facility","World Bank","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The Global Infrastructure Facility is associated with the World Bank Group.\nIt supports infrastructure development and helps mobilise investment.\nIt has a strong focus on public-private partnership (PPP) projects.\nTherefore, option (b) is correct.","revisionPoints":"GIF → Global Infrastructure Facility.\nAssociated with the World Bank.\nSupports infrastructure investment.\nImportant focus → PPP infrastructure projects.\nHelps mobilise public and private capital."},
{"no":57,"text":"For election to the Lok Sabha, a nomination paper can be filed by:","A":"Anyone residing in India","B":"A resident of the constituency","C":"Any citizen of India whose name appears in the electoral roll of a constituency","D":"Any citizen of India","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Elections","tags":["Lok Sabha Nomination","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The source identifies the requirement as being an Indian citizen whose name appears in an electoral roll.\nA candidate does not necessarily have to be a resident of the constituency from which they contest.\nHence, residence in the particular constituency is not the deciding requirement.","revisionPoints":"Lok Sabha candidate → must satisfy constitutional/statutory qualifications.\nCitizenship of India is essential.\nCandidate need not necessarily be a resident of the constituency.\nElection law also prescribes age and other qualification requirements."},
{"no":58,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe Himalayas are spread over five States.\nThe Western Ghats are spread over five States only.\nPulicat Lake is spread over two States only.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Himalayas","Western Ghats","Pulicat Lake","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The Himalayas extend across more than five States/UTs.\nThe Western Ghats extend across six States according to the source.\nPulicat Lake lies across Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu.\nTherefore, only statement 3 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Pulicat Lake → Andhra Pradesh + Tamil Nadu.\nIt is one of India's important brackish water lagoons.\nWestern Ghats span multiple states along western peninsular India.\nHimalayas extend across a broad arc in northern India."},
{"no":59,"text":"Biological Oxygen Demand (BOD) is a standard criterion for:","A":"Measuring oxygen levels in blood","B":"Computing oxygen levels in forests","C":"Pollution assay in aquatic ecosystems","D":"Assessing oxygen levels in high-altitude regions","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Biological Oxygen Demand","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"BOD measures the amount of dissolved oxygen consumed by microorganisms while decomposing organic matter in water.\nHigher BOD generally indicates a greater load of biodegradable organic pollution.\nTherefore, BOD is an important indicator of water pollution.","revisionPoints":"BOD = Biological Oxygen Demand.\nHigh BOD → generally indicates higher organic pollution.\nDissolved oxygen is consumed during decomposition.\nBOD is widely used in assessment of water quality.\nDistinguish from COD – Chemical Oxygen Demand."},
{"no":60,"text":"With reference to UN-Habitat, which statements are correct?\nUN-Habitat promotes socially and environmentally sustainable towns and cities.\nIts partners are either governments or local urban authorities only.\nIt contributes to reducing poverty and promoting access to safe drinking water and sanitation.","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1 only","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["UN-Habitat","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"UN-Habitat works towards socially and environmentally sustainable human settlements.\nIt also contributes to goals related to poverty reduction, water and sanitation.\nIts partnerships extend beyond governments and urban authorities.\nIt also works with NGOs, civil society and the private sector.","revisionPoints":"UN-Habitat → United Nations Human Settlements Programme.\nFocus → sustainable cities and human settlements.\nImportant areas:\nUrbanisation\nHousing\nWater and sanitation\nPoverty reduction\nWorks with governments, civil society, NGOs and private-sector stakeholders.\nUPSC – Questions 61–80"},
{"no":61,"text":"With reference to the National Skills Qualification Framework (NSQF), consider the following:\nCertification can be acquired only through formal learning.\nIt promotes mobility between vocational and general education.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Labour & Employment","tags":["National Skills Qualification Framework","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"NSQF provides a framework for organising qualifications according to learning outcomes and competencies.\nCertification is not restricted only to formal learning.\nIt promotes mobility between vocational education and general education.\nTherefore, only statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"NSQF → National Skills Qualification Framework.\nFocuses on learning outcomes and competency levels.\nSupports mobility between vocational and general education.\nHelps standardise skill qualifications."},
{"no":62,"text":"In Indian history, the principle of \"Dyarchy (Diarchy)\" refers to:","A":"Division of the Central Legislature","B":"Double government at Centre and States","C":"Two rulers (London and Delhi)","D":"Division of provincial subjects into Reserved and Transferred subjects","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Dyarchy","Government of India Act 1919","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Dyarchy was introduced in the provinces under the Government of India Act, 1919.\nProvincial subjects were divided into:\nReserved subjects\nTransferred subjects\nReserved subjects remained under the Governor and his executive councillors.\nTransferred subjects were administered by ministers responsible to the legislature.","revisionPoints":"Dyarchy → Government of India Act, 1919.\nApplied at the provincial level.\nReserved subjects → Governor/executive councillors.\nTransferred subjects → Ministers responsible to legislature.\nProvincial dyarchy was later abolished by the Government of India Act, 1935."},
{"no":63,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nNational Career Service is an initiative of the Department of Personnel & Training.\nIt was launched to improve employment opportunities.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Labour & Employment","tags":["National Career Service","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"National Career Service aims to improve employment and career-related services.\nIt comes under the Ministry of Labour & Employment, not the Department of Personnel & Training.\nHence, statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"NCS → National Career Service.\nMinistry → Labour & Employment.\nProvides employment and career-related services.\nConnects job seekers and employers."},
{"no":64,"text":"The Scheme for Sustainable Structuring of Stressed Assets (S4A) is:","A":"Environmental scheme","B":"RBI scheme for restructuring stressed corporate loans","C":"PSU disinvestment plan","D":"Insolvency Code provision","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["S4A","Stressed Assets","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"S4A was introduced by the Reserve Bank of India.\nIt dealt with restructuring of stressed corporate loans.\nThe objective was to address financial stress in viable projects while improving the resolution of stressed assets.\nTherefore, option (b) is correct.","revisionPoints":"S4A → Scheme for Sustainable Structuring of Stressed Assets.\nRBI framework.\nRelated to stressed corporate debt.\nPart of India's broader efforts to address NPAs and stressed assets."},
{"no":65,"text":"Regarding the Climate and Clean Air Coalition (CCAC):\nIt is a G20 initiative.\nIt focuses on methane, black carbon and hydrofluorocarbons.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Climate and Clean Air Coalition","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"CCAC focuses on reducing short-lived climate pollutants.\nImportant pollutants include:\nMethane\nBlack carbon\nHydrofluorocarbons\nIt is not a G20 initiative.\nTherefore, only statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"CCAC → Climate and Clean Air Coalition.\nFocus → short-lived climate pollutants (SLCPs).\nMajor targets → methane, black carbon, HFCs.\nClimate policy can address both long-lived greenhouse gases and SLCPs."},
{"no":66,"text":"Regarding the Indian Ocean Dipole (IOD):\nIt is characterized by a difference in sea surface temperature between the western Indian Ocean and the eastern Indian Ocean.\nIt can influence the impact of El Niño on the Indian monsoon.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Indian Ocean Dipole","Monsoon","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"IOD is associated with differences in sea-surface temperatures between the western and eastern tropical Indian Ocean.\nIt influences atmospheric circulation over the Indian Ocean region.\nIts positive or negative phases can affect the Indian monsoon.\nIt can also modify the influence of El Niño on monsoon rainfall.","revisionPoints":"IOD → Indian Ocean Dipole.\nPositive IOD → relatively warmer western Indian Ocean.\nNegative IOD → relatively warmer eastern Indian Ocean.\nIOD interacts with ENSO/El Niño.\nImportant factor in Indian monsoon variability."},
{"no":67,"text":"If you want to see gharials in their natural habitat, which is the best place?","A":"Bhitarkanika Mangroves","B":"Chambal River","C":"Pulicat Lake","D":"Deepor Beel","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Gharial","Chambal River","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The Chambal River is an important natural habitat of the gharial.\nThe National Chambal Sanctuary provides protected riverine habitat.\nGharials are specialised fish-eating crocodilians adapted to river systems.\nHence, Chambal River is the correct choice.","revisionPoints":"Gharial → Gavialis gangeticus.\nHighly specialised riverine crocodilian.\nMajor habitat → Chambal River.\nThreats include habitat alteration, fishing pressure and changes in river flow."},
{"no":68,"text":"Regarding the Indian Ocean Naval Symposium (IONS):\nThe inaugural IONS was held in India in 2008 under the Indian Navy.\nIONS seeks to increase maritime cooperation among navies of the Indian Ocean Region.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["Indian Ocean Naval Symposium","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The inaugural IONS was held in India in 2008.\nIt was initiated by the Indian Navy.\nIONS aims to strengthen maritime cooperation and information exchange among Indian Ocean regional navies.\nTherefore, both statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"IONS → Indian Ocean Naval Symposium.\nInaugural meeting → 2008, India.\nFocus → maritime cooperation.\nCovers countries of the Indian Ocean Region.\nIt is a naval cooperation forum, not a military alliance."},
{"no":69,"text":"The painting of Bodhisattva Padmapani is one of the most famous paintings at:","A":"Ajanta","B":"Badami","C":"Bagh","D":"Ellora","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Painting","tags":["Ajanta","Bodhisattva Padmapani","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The famous Bodhisattva Padmapani painting is located in the Ajanta Caves.\nAjanta is renowned for its Buddhist murals and rock-cut architecture.\nPadmapani is generally depicted holding a lotus.\nAjanta's paintings belong broadly to the Buddhist artistic tradition.","revisionPoints":"Ajanta → Maharashtra.\nFamous for Buddhist cave paintings.\nPadmapani → Bodhisattva associated with compassion.\nAjanta caves are associated mainly with Buddhist traditions.\nAjanta and Ellora are both UNESCO World Heritage Sites but represent different religious/artistic traditions."},
{"no":70,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nTradition\nCommunity\n1. Chaliha Sahib Festival\nSindhis\n2. Nanda Raj Jaat Yatra\nGonds\n3. Wari-Warkari\nSanthals","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"None of the above","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Festivals & Traditions","tags":["Chaliha Sahib","Nanda Raj Jaat","Wari-Warkari","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Chaliha Sahib Festival is associated with the Sindhi community.\nNanda Raj Jaat Yatra is associated with Uttarakhand, not the Gond community.\nWari-Warkari tradition is associated with Maharashtra, not the Santhals.\nTherefore, only pair 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Chaliha Sahib → Sindhi community.\nNanda Raj Jaat → Uttarakhand.\nWari-Warkari → Maharashtra.\nCultural questions often require linking festival + region + community."},
{"no":71,"text":"Which of the following practices can help in water conservation in agriculture?\nReduced/Zero tillage\nApplying gypsum before irrigation\nAllowing crop residue to remain in the field","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Water Conservation","Zero Tillage","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Reduced or zero tillage helps retain soil moisture.\nCrop residue acts as mulch and reduces evaporation and soil moisture loss.\nGypsum is mainly used for improving certain sodic/alkali soils, rather than being a general water-conservation practice.\nTherefore, statements 1 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Zero tillage → minimum soil disturbance.\nCrop residue → mulching effect.\nBenefits include:\nMoisture conservation\nReduced erosion\nBetter soil structure\nGypsum → particularly useful for sodic soils."},
{"no":72,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe Soil Health Card Scheme aims at:\nExpanding irrigated area.\nEnabling banks to assess loan eligibility.\nChecking overuse of fertilizers.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Soil Health Card Scheme","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Soil Health Cards provide information about soil nutrient status.\nThis helps farmers apply fertilisers according to actual soil requirements.\nThus, the scheme can help prevent excessive or inappropriate fertiliser use.\nIt is not primarily intended to expand irrigation or determine bank loan eligibility.","revisionPoints":"Soil Health Card → soil nutrient assessment.\nHelps determine appropriate fertiliser application.\nPromotes balanced fertilisation.\nSupports sustainable agricultural productivity."},
{"no":73,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nMaterial\nUndesirable Chemical\n1. Lipstick\nLead\n2. Soft drinks\nBrominated Vegetable Oils\n3. Chinese fast food\nMonosodium Glutamate (MSG)","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Materials Science","tags":["Lead","Brominated Vegetable Oils","MSG","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The source identifies all three pairs as correct.\nLead has historically been discussed as a contaminant of concern in some cosmetic products.\nBrominated vegetable oils have been used as beverage additives, though their regulatory status varies.\nMSG is a flavour enhancer commonly associated with processed foods.","revisionPoints":"Lead → toxic heavy metal.\nBrominated Vegetable Oil (BVO) → beverage additive.\nMSG → flavour enhancer.\nFood and cosmetic safety standards regulate permitted substances and concentrations."},
{"no":74,"text":"Organic Light Emitting Diodes (OLEDs) are used to create digital displays. What are their advantages over LCDs?\nOLED displays can be fabricated on flexible plastic substrates.\nRoll-up displays embedded in clothing can be made using OLEDs.\nTransparent displays are possible using OLEDs.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"None of the above","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["OLED","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"OLEDs are based on light-emitting organic materials.\nUnlike conventional LCDs, OLED panels can be made thin and flexible.\nThis allows applications such as rollable or wearable displays.\nOLED technology can also be used to create transparent display systems.","revisionPoints":"OLED → Organic Light Emitting Diode.\nDoes not require a conventional backlight like an LCD.\nAdvantages:\nThin\nFlexible\nHigh contrast\nPotentially transparent/rollable\nUsed in smartphones, televisions and wearable technology."},
{"no":75,"text":"Which of the following are famous for Sun Temples?\nArasavalli\nAmarkantak\nOmkareshwar","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Temples & Shrines","tags":["Sun Temples","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Arasavalli in Andhra Pradesh is famous for its Sun Temple dedicated to Surya.\nAmarkantak is primarily associated with important Hindu pilgrimage sites and the origin of rivers.\nOmkareshwar is particularly famous for the Jyotirlinga temple dedicated to Shiva.\nTherefore, only Arasavalli is correct.","revisionPoints":"Arasavalli Sun Temple → Andhra Pradesh.\nDedicated to Surya.\nOmkareshwar → Madhya Pradesh → Jyotirlinga of Shiva.\nAmarkantak → Madhya Pradesh → source region of Narmada and Son rivers."},
{"no":76,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nA winning candidate in Lok Sabha/Assembly elections must secure at least 50% of the votes polled.\nThe Constitution provides that the Speaker belongs to the ruling party and the Deputy Speaker to the Opposition.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Elections","tags":["Speaker","Deputy Speaker","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"India generally follows the first-past-the-post system.\nA candidate does not need to secure 50% of votes; the candidate with the highest number of votes wins.\nThe Constitution does not mandate that the Speaker must belong to the ruling party or the Deputy Speaker to the Opposition.\nTherefore, both statements are incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Lok Sabha elections → First-Past-the-Post (FPTP).\nWinning candidate need not obtain 50%+1 votes.\nSpeaker → elected by the House.\nConstitution does not prescribe a party affiliation for Speaker/Deputy Speaker."},
{"no":77,"text":"Which of the following occurred in India after the 1991 economic reforms?\nShare of agriculture in GDP increased enormously.\nIndia's share in world exports increased.\nFDI inflows increased.\nForeign exchange reserves increased enormously.","A":"1 and 4 only","B":"2, 3 and 4 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["1991 Economic Reforms","LPG Reforms","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Economic reforms increased India's integration with the global economy.\nIndia's participation in world trade increased.\nForeign Direct Investment inflows expanded.\nIndia's foreign exchange reserves increased substantially over the reform period.\nThe share of agriculture in GDP did not increase enormously; it declined relatively as other sectors expanded.","revisionPoints":"1991 reforms → Liberalisation, Privatisation, Globalisation (LPG).\nMajor changes:\nTrade liberalisation\nFDI liberalisation\nIndustrial deregulation\nFinancial-sector reforms\nAgriculture's relative GDP share declined over time.\nIndia's integration with the global economy increased."},
{"no":78,"text":"What is the application of Somatic Cell Nuclear Transfer (SCNT)?","A":"Production of biolarvicides","B":"Manufacture of biodegradable plastics","C":"Reproductive cloning of animals","D":"Production of disease-free organisms","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Somatic Cell Nuclear Transfer","Cloning","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"SCNT involves transferring the nucleus of a somatic cell into an enucleated egg cell.\nThe resulting embryo can be developed into an organism genetically similar to the nuclear donor.\nIt is therefore associated with reproductive cloning.\nDolly the sheep is the famous example of SCNT-based cloning.","revisionPoints":"SCNT → Somatic Cell Nuclear Transfer.\nBasic steps:\nRemove nucleus from egg cell.\nInsert somatic-cell nucleus.\nStimulate cell division.\nDevelop embryo.\nFamous example → Dolly sheep.\nRelated to cloning technology."},
{"no":79,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nNPCI helps promote financial inclusion.\nNPCI launched RuPay.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["NPCI","RuPay","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"NPCI plays a major role in India's retail digital payments infrastructure.\nIt supports financial inclusion through payment systems.\nNPCI launched RuPay, India's domestic card payment network.\nHence, both statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"NPCI → National Payments Corporation of India.\nKey systems associated with NPCI include:\nUPI\nRuPay\nIMPS\nBharat BillPay\nRuPay → domestic card payment network.\nNPCI plays an important role in digital financial inclusion."},
{"no":80,"text":"The term \"M-STrIPES\" refers to:","A":"Captive breeding of wild fauna","B":"Maintenance and monitoring of Tiger Reserves","C":"Indigenous satellite navigation","D":"Security of National Highways","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["M-STrIPES","Tiger Reserves","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"M-STrIPES is associated with monitoring and management of tiger reserves.\nIt uses technology and field-based information to improve monitoring.\nIt can assist in recording patrol information, wildlife observations and management activities.\nTherefore, option (b) is correct.","revisionPoints":"M-STrIPES → Monitoring System for Tigers – Intensive Protection and Ecological Status.\nUsed in tiger reserve management.\nSupports:\nPatrol monitoring\nWildlife monitoring\nProtection activities\nEcological assessment\nTechnology is used to strengthen wildlife protection and management.\nUPSC – Questions 81–100"},
{"no":81,"text":"Which of the following are likely advantages of GST?\nReplacement of multiple indirect taxes by one market.\nReduction of Current Account Deficit.\nBoost to economic growth.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["GST","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"GST replaced several indirect taxes with a more integrated indirect tax framework.\nIt aims to create a more unified national market.\nGST can improve efficiency and potentially support economic growth.\nReduction of the Current Account Deficit is not a direct or necessary consequence of GST.","revisionPoints":"GST → Goods and Services Tax.\nImplemented in India from 1 July 2017.\nMajor objective → create a common national market.\nDestination-based indirect tax.\nIncludes CGST, SGST/UTGST and IGST."},
{"no":82,"text":"\"Broad-based Trade and Investment Agreement (BTIA)\" negotiations are between India and:","A":"European Union","B":"Gulf Cooperation Council","C":"OECD","D":"Shanghai Cooperation Organisation","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["BTIA","India-EU Relations","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"BTIA negotiations refer to the proposed India–European Union trade and investment agreement.\nIt covers areas relating to trade in goods, services and investment.\nTherefore, the correct answer is the European Union.","revisionPoints":"BTIA → Broad-based Trade and Investment Agreement.\nNegotiations → India and EU.\nBroad areas include:\nTrade in goods\nServices\nInvestment\nIndia-EU trade relations are part of India's wider economic diplomacy."},
{"no":83,"text":"Regarding the WTO Trade Facilitation Agreement (TFA):\nIndia has ratified it.\nIt is part of the Bali Package (2013).\nIt came into force in January 2016.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["WTO","Trade Facilitation Agreement","Bali Package","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"India ratified the WTO Trade Facilitation Agreement.\nThe TFA was part of the Bali Package adopted in 2013.\nThe source marks the statement regarding it coming into force in January 2016 as incorrect.\nTherefore, statements 1 and 2 are correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"TFA → Trade Facilitation Agreement.\nAdopted as part of the Bali Package, 2013.\nFocuses on simplifying and harmonising customs procedures.\nIntended to reduce delays and transaction costs in international trade."},
{"no":84,"text":"What is the importance of Chabahar Port for India?","A":"Increase trade with Africa","B":"Strengthen ties with Arab countries","C":"Access Afghanistan and Central Asia without depending on Pakistan","D":"Gas pipeline through Pakistan","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Chabahar Port","Iran","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Chabahar Port is located in Iran.\nIt provides India with an alternative connectivity route towards Afghanistan and Central Asia.\nThis route reduces India's dependence on land routes through Pakistan.\nIt is therefore strategically important for India's regional connectivity.","revisionPoints":"Chabahar Port → Iran.\nImportant for India's connectivity with:\nAfghanistan\nCentral Asia\nProvides an alternative to routes through Pakistan.\nConnected with India's broader regional connectivity strategy."},
{"no":85,"text":"Reporting cyber security incidents is legally mandatory for:\nService providers\nData centres\nBody corporate","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Cyber Security","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The source identifies all three categories as subject to mandatory cyber-security incident reporting requirements.\nSuch reporting helps authorities respond to and investigate cyber incidents.\nTherefore, statements 1, 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Cyber incidents can affect:\nService providers\nData centres\nCompanies/body corporates\nIncident reporting supports cybersecurity response.\nCERT-In plays an important role in India's cyber incident response framework.\nTimely reporting helps in threat identification and mitigation."},
{"no":86,"text":"Right to vote and to be elected in India is a:","A":"Fundamental Right","B":"Natural Right","C":"Constitutional Right","D":"Legal Right","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Elections","tags":["Right to Vote","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The right to vote is not classified as a Fundamental Right.\nElectoral rights are governed primarily by constitutional provisions and election laws.\nThe source categorises the right to vote and contest elections as a legal right.","revisionPoints":"Right to vote → not a Fundamental Right.\nElectoral rights are governed by election laws.\nFundamental Rights are mainly contained in Part III.\nUniversal adult suffrage is provided through the constitutional electoral framework."},
{"no":87,"text":"The purpose of the eLISA project is:","A":"Detect neutrinos","B":"Detect gravitational waves","C":"Test missiles","D":"Study solar flares","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["eLISA","Gravitational Waves","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"eLISA refers to a proposed space-based gravitational-wave observatory concept.\nIt would use interferometric measurements to detect gravitational waves.\nSpace-based detection complements ground-based gravitational-wave observatories.\nTherefore, option (b) is correct.","revisionPoints":"eLISA → space-based gravitational-wave detection concept.\nGravitational waves are ripples in spacetime.\nSources can include:\nMerging black holes\nCompact stellar objects\nSpace observatories can probe frequencies difficult to detect from Earth."},
{"no":88,"text":"Vidyanjali Yojana aims to:\nPermit foreign universities to open campuses.\nImprove government schools through community participation.\nEncourage voluntary monetary contributions.","A":"2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"2 and 3 only","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Vidyanjali Yojana","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Vidyanjali aims to improve government schools through community participation.\nIt encourages volunteers and professionals to contribute their knowledge, skills and support.\nThe source does not treat opening foreign university campuses or voluntary monetary contributions as the objectives tested here.","revisionPoints":"Vidyanjali → school education initiative.\nFocus → participation of community/volunteers.\nIntended to strengthen government schools.\nNot a foreign-university campus scheme."},
{"no":89,"text":"Unnat Bharat Abhiyan aims at:","A":"100% literacy","B":"Connecting higher education institutions with villages","C":"Strengthening research institutions","D":"Human capital through welfare funds","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Unnat Bharat Abhiyan","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Unnat Bharat Abhiyan seeks to connect higher educational institutions with rural communities.\nInstitutions work with villages to understand local problems and develop appropriate solutions.\nIt promotes rural development through knowledge, technology and community engagement.","revisionPoints":"Unnat Bharat Abhiyan → rural development.\nLinks higher educational institutions and villages.\nFocuses on locally relevant development solutions.\nEncourages participation of students, faculty and rural communities."},
{"no":90,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nElection Commission is a five-member body.\nMHA decides election schedules.\nElection Commission decides disputes regarding recognized political parties.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"3 only","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Elections","tags":["Election Commission","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The Election Commission of India is not constitutionally fixed as a five-member body.\nElection schedules are decided by the Election Commission, not the Ministry of Home Affairs.\nThe Election Commission has powers relating to disputes concerning recognition of political parties and allocation of election symbols.\nTherefore, statement 3 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Election Commission → Article 324.\nResponsible for superintendence, direction and control of elections.\nDeals with:\nElection schedules\nPolitical party recognition\nElection symbols\nElection Commission is an independent constitutional authority."},
{"no":91,"text":"If a tortoise is included in Schedule I of the Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972, it implies:","A":"Same level of protection as tiger.","B":"Extinct in the wild.","C":"Endemic.","D":"Both B and C.","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Law","tags":["Wildlife Protection Act","Schedule I","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Schedule I provides a very high level of legal protection to listed wildlife.\nSpecies in this category receive strong protection against hunting and related offences.\nInclusion in Schedule I does not mean that the species is extinct or endemic.\nTherefore, the correct answer is option (a).","revisionPoints":"Schedule I → highest level of protection under the framework referenced in the question.\nHunting is generally prohibited except under specified legal circumstances.\nSchedule status does not automatically indicate:\nEndemism\nExtinction\nWildlife protection status and conservation status are different concepts."},
{"no":92,"text":"Judicial Review in India means:","A":"Judiciary can examine constitutionality of laws and executive actions.","B":"Judiciary questions legislative wisdom.","C":"Judiciary reviews Bills before assent.","D":"Judiciary reviews only its own judgments.","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Judicial Review","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Judicial review allows courts to examine whether laws and executive actions are consistent with the Constitution.\nIf a law violates constitutional provisions, courts can declare it invalid to the extent of the inconsistency.\nIt is an important mechanism for maintaining constitutional supremacy.","revisionPoints":"Judicial Review → protects constitutional supremacy.\nCourts can examine:\nLegislative Acts\nExecutive actions\nConstitutional validity\nIt is different from merely questioning the wisdom or desirability of legislation.\nSupreme Court and High Courts exercise judicial review."},
{"no":93,"text":"Arrange chronologically:\nRoyal Indian Navy Mutiny\nQuit India Movement\nSecond Round Table Conference","A":"1-2-3","B":"2-1-3","C":"3-2-1","D":"3-1-2","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Royal Indian Navy Mutiny","Quit India Movement","Round Table Conference","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Second Round Table Conference → 1931\nQuit India Movement → 1942\nRoyal Indian Navy Mutiny → 1946\nTherefore, the correct chronological order is 3 → 2 → 1.","revisionPoints":"Second Round Table Conference → 1931.\nQuit India Movement → 1942.\nRoyal Indian Navy Mutiny → 1946.\nChronology is an important technique for modern Indian history questions."},
{"no":94,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nTax revenue as % of GDP steadily increased during the last decade.\nFiscal deficit as % of GDP steadily increased during the last decade.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Tax Revenue","Fiscal Deficit","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Neither tax revenue as a percentage of GDP nor fiscal deficit as a percentage of GDP followed a steady, uninterrupted increase over the period referred to.\nBoth indicators can fluctuate because of economic cycles, policy changes and extraordinary events.\nTherefore, neither statement is correct.","revisionPoints":"Fiscal deficit → excess of total expenditure over total receipts excluding borrowings.\nTax-to-GDP ratio → tax revenue relative to economic output.\nFiscal indicators can vary with:\nEconomic growth\nTax policy\nGovernment expenditure\nEconomic shocks"},
{"no":95,"text":"Lions from Gujarat were proposed to be translocated to:","A":"Corbett National Park","B":"Kuno Palpur Wildlife Sanctuary","C":"Mudumalai Wildlife Sanctuary","D":"Sariska National Park","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Asiatic Lion","Kuno Palpur","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Kuno Palpur in Madhya Pradesh was identified as a potential site for establishing an additional population of Asiatic lions.\nThe proposal was aimed at reducing the risks associated with having the lion population concentrated in a single geographical area.\nThe source identifies Kuno Palpur as the proposed destination.","revisionPoints":"Asiatic lion → primarily associated with Gujarat's Gir landscape.\nKuno → Madhya Pradesh.\nTranslocation can help establish additional populations.\nConservation principle → reduce risk from disease or other catastrophic events affecting a single population."},
{"no":96,"text":"Which are not necessarily consequences of President's Rule?\nDissolution of Legislative Assembly\nRemoval of Council of Ministers\nDissolution of Local Bodies","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["President’s Rule","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Under President's Rule, the President can assume functions of the State Government.\nThe Council of Ministers ceases to function because the State executive is taken over.\nDissolution of the Legislative Assembly is not automatic; it may be kept in suspended animation.\nDissolution of local bodies is also not a necessary consequence.","revisionPoints":"President's Rule → Article 356.\nImposed when constitutional machinery in a State fails.\nState Council of Ministers → ceases to function.\nLegislative Assembly → may be:\nDissolved, or\nKept in suspended animation.\nLocal bodies are not automatically dissolved."},
{"no":97,"text":"The Right against Exploitation includes:\nProhibition of trafficking and forced labour\nAbolition of untouchability\nProtection of minorities\nProhibition of child labour in factories and mines","A":"1, 2 and 4 only","B":"2, 3 and 4 only","C":"1 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Right against Exploitation","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Article 23 prohibits trafficking in human beings and forced labour.\nArticle 24 prohibits employment of children below 14 years in factories, mines and hazardous employment.\nAbolition of untouchability comes under Article 17.\nProtection of minorities is associated with other Fundamental Rights provisions.","revisionPoints":"Right against Exploitation → Articles 23–24.\nArticle 23 → trafficking and forced labour.\nArticle 24 → child labour in factories, mines and hazardous employment.\nArticle 17 → abolition of untouchability.\nFundamental Rights are grouped by subject in Part III."},
{"no":98,"text":"Which place is geographically closest to Great Nicobar?","A":"Sumatra","B":"Borneo","C":"Java","D":"Sri Lanka","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Great Nicobar","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"Great Nicobar lies at the southern end of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.\nGeographically, it is much closer to Sumatra in Indonesia than to the other options.\nThe island lies near the northern entrance to the Strait of Malacca region.","revisionPoints":"Great Nicobar → Andaman & Nicobar Islands.\nSouthernmost major island of the Indian island group.\nClosest among the given options → Sumatra.\nImportant geographically because of its proximity to Southeast Asia and major sea routes."},
{"no":99,"text":"The Cabinet form of Government is based on:","A":"Reducing criticism","B":"Speeding administration","C":"Collective responsibility of the Government to the Legislature","D":"Strengthening the Prime Minister","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Cabinet Government","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"The cabinet system is fundamentally based on collective responsibility.\nThe Council of Ministers acts collectively and is responsible to the Lok Sabha.\nIf the government loses the confidence of the House, the Council of Ministers is expected to resign.\nTherefore, option (c) is correct.","revisionPoints":"Article 75(3): Council of Ministers collectively responsible to Lok Sabha.\nCabinet government → parliamentary form.\nPrime Minister leads the Council of Ministers.\nCollective responsibility promotes unity and legislative accountability."},
{"no":100,"text":"Which one of the following is not a feature of Indian federalism?","A":"Independent Judiciary","B":"Clear division of powers","C":"Unequal representation of States in Rajya Sabha","D":"It is the result of an agreement among the federating units","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Indian Federalism","UPSC","2017"],"expl":"India has a federal structure with unitary features.\nThere is a constitutional division of powers between the Union and States.\nIndia has an independent judiciary to adjudicate constitutional disputes.\nUnlike classical federations formed by an agreement among independent States, India is described as a \"Union of States\" under Article 1.","revisionPoints":"India → Union of States.\nFederal features:\nWritten Constitution\nDivision of powers\nIndependent judiciary\nConstitutional supremacy\nIndian federalism also has unitary features.\nRajya Sabha representation of States is not equal; it broadly reflects population differences."},
];
